        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: keep tail alive and replay timed-out messages",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nA Discord Gateway event handler error, recovered panic, or cooperative timeout\npreviously shared the same fatal path as worker-queue saturation. Keeping the\ntail alive exposed a second loss mode: a timed-out message can remain absent\nafter the channel cursor advances beyond it, so ordinary forward repair can no\nlonger fetch that exact message.\n\nThe recovery path also needs to fail closed when an ordered handler ignores\ncancellation. Otherwise the timed-out handler can continue writing while later\nordered events begin, and the exact failed message may not be durably recorded\nbefore the tail exits.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\n- Separate ordinary event-scoped failures from fatal tail failures while\n  retaining bounded three-consecutive-failure escalation.\n- Serialize message and channel events on one ordered worker while member work\n  uses separate workers and a separate failure circuit.\n- Treat a genuinely detached ordered timeout as immediately fatal, stop later\n  ordered dequeue, and preserve that fatal result across concurrent shutdown.\n- Persist the exact timed-out message identity transactionally before fatal\n  return; surface persistence failures in the supervisor-visible error.\n- Carry safe event metadata through queued work and reject conflicting\n  identities returned by message-update refetches.\n- Process bounded unresolved `discord/tail_message` failures through the\n  maintenance-only replay path after ordinary forward repair.\n- Fetch each replay candidate by exact channel/message ID, validate identity,\n  and persist through the canonical idempotent message upsert path.\n- Resolve the exact failure only after durable canonical presence. Missing,\n  inaccessible, mismatched, and retryable responses remain unresolved.\n- Keep replay guild-scoped, exclusion-aware, bounded, idempotent, and free of\n  synthetic `message_events`, publishing, cursor regression, or\n  `tail:last_event` updates.\n\n## User Impact\n\nTransient per-event failures no longer stop a healthy Gateway tail. A\nnon-cooperative ordered handler cannot overlap later ordered work, and its exact\nmessage identity is durably retained before the supervisor restarts the tail.\nHourly maintenance can recover a failed message behind its channel cursor\nwithout replaying channel history or duplicating Gateway events.\n\nWarnings and replay summaries contain identifiers and classifications only;\nthey do not log message content, payloads, usernames, tokens, panic values, or\nraw secret-bearing errors.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent head: `c3e236905f6a4ef4ea05d6c4303340ee24630fb0`\n\n- `go test -count=1 ./...`\n- Filtered repository coverage: `85.1%` against the `85.0%` floor\n- `go test -count=1 -race ./...`\n- Focused timeout boundary tests: 50 normal and 20 race repetitions\n- `go vet ./...`\n- `golangci-lint`, `staticcheck`, `deadcode`, `gofumpt`, and `gosec`\n- `govulncheck ./...`\n- Build and CLI control-surface smoke checks\n- Independent Pinchy review: `ZERO BLOCKERS`\n\nFocused tests cover detached-timeout ordering, durable failure persistence,\nrecorder failure, parent-shutdown races, cancellation-grace boundaries,\nbelow-cursor recovery, monotonic cursors, idempotence, no synthetic events,\nunchanged `tail:last_event`, bounded retry rotation, guild scope, channel\nexclusions, normal-page resolution, inaccessible responses, and identity\nmismatches.\n",
        "branch": "codex/fix-tail-handler-timeouts",
